{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q3: the interval clock is FIXED RATE -- `each successive interval arrives n ticks after the previous interval, on that original schedule. The period is never re-measured from the moment an invocation happens to finish` (spec/interaction-events.md:229-233). A one-time block is what separates the two readings: a key press at tick 4 holds the thread for six ticks while the `every 4` handler is claimed, so the handler is delayed but its clock is not. Measured: intervals arrive at ticks 4, 8, 12 and 16 -- the original grid, which the `on_key` body's nested `wait 6` extends the run past by advancing the shared clock -- and the run prints FOUR times. Under the rejected fixed-DELAY reading the period restarts from each invocation's completion instead, so the grid slips off those boundaries and the same program prints THREE times. Fixed delay is also self-defeating: it disarms the handler for the duration of an invocation, so a new interval could never arrive while one was running, and the queueing sentence at :235-242 would be dead text -- which is the argument that settles the question. Dropping the missed occurrence also prints 3 here, so this fixture is not by itself a proof of fixed rate; it is the `every-*-queued-and-runs` pair that separates those two, and this one that separates fixed rate from fixed delay.",
